# Think ahead — don't wait to be told

When the user asks for a task, also do the **adjacent deliverables** a careful researcher would want next, without asking first (unless expensive/destructive).

## Before finishing any training / model / dataset job

Ask yourself: "If they come back in an hour, what will they wish I had saved?"

Default checklist (do all that apply):

- **Checkpoints** — `torch.save` / equivalent per fold or best epoch (`state_dict`, arch, hyperparams, held-out split id)
- **Intermediate features** — pooled / encoder embeddings the head sits on (OOF when using LORO), not just logits
- **Predictions + metrics** — OOF preds, per-fold and mean scores, comparison vs baseline
- **Plots** — write PNGs under `outputs/` and embed as markdown in the notebook so they can view without rerun
- **Manifest** — short JSON: paths, dims, seed, epochs, device, how to reload
- **Concurrency** — use available GPUs/CPUs when folds/tiles are independent (see parallel-concurrency rule)
- **Progress** — `tqdm` + unbuffered logs on long jobs

## Anti-patterns

```text
❌ Train a model → only print F1 → discard weights
❌ Build variable-size inputs for a CNN → never cache fixed-size encoder vectors
❌ Generate plots in a script run → leave notebook empty so user must retrain to see them
❌ Single-GPU loop when 8 idle GPUs and independent folds exist
```

```text
✅ Train → save checkpoints + OOF embeddings + metrics CSV + plots + snapshot.json
✅ After "train CNN" assume UMAP/EDA on the pooled layer may be next → cache it
✅ Notebook shows results from outputs/ via markdown images without re-execution
```

## Scope judgment

- Do proactive extras when cheap relative to the main job (saving a `.pt` during training is mandatory).
- If an extra would **double+ wall time** or spend money, do the cheap saves now and note the optional heavier follow-up in one line.
- Prefer one complete pipeline over a minimal answer that forces a redo.
